  "result": "I rewrote `record.jsonl` with the revised node record. Nothing has checked it yet; the validator and the gate run next.\n\n- **Validator defect (s5 carried twice):** s5 now has only one carrier, the declared intention `int.left_turn`. The atoms for where the turn happens stay on s2.\n- **Gate verdict (the turn-target variables `i`/`d` not tied to the subject):** s2 now also claims `st.on_way` and `st.highway`. The applicability now says the intersection or driveway/alley entrance must lie on a highway that a roadway the subject occupies also lies on. I wrote this down as what \"preparing to turn left\" refers to: a left turn off a roadway is made where it meets another way, and that place lies on the roadway's highway. The coder had refused to make this change because it treated it as narrowing an exception, which is not allowed. If the gate takes that view too, the record will come back on that point. When the coder tried this tie once as a test, the gate stopped complaining about unanchored variables.\n- **Branches 1–4:** each now expects `not_applicable`. The applicability says the duty does not arise when an exception holds, so these scenes, where an exception holds, cannot be `compliant`. I also fixed branch_1's narrative so it no longer contradicts itself.\n- **Branch_5:** I replaced the electric-scooter scene. Its subject can never be decided a member of the subject class, because the electric-scooter aggregate leaves the scooter class unresolved. The new branch_5 expects `violated`: a left turn is declared at an intersection on a different highway, so exception (b) does not hold. It tests the new tie.\n- **Not covered:** no scenario now shows the \"lawful speed\" part of exception (a) failing. The only subject it can catch is an electric scooter, and a scooter subject can only come out unknown until the aggregate's reading is settled.",
